WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
Cloud incident response combines traditional incident response discipline with

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.180
cloud-native concerns.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.480
The familiar phases of detection, analysis, containment, recovery,

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.460
and lessons learned still apply,

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:18.920
but the evidence and control points often involve identities, APIs, audit logs, policies,

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.740
storage, managed services, workloads, automation, and provider boundaries.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.520
Strong response depends on preparation.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:33.900
Teams need asset inventory, identity inventory, logging, centralized evidence, retention,

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:40.120
runbooks, scoped access, response roles, and escalation paths before a serious incident.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.060
Without visibility, responders may be forced to make decisions from incomplete evidence.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.440
With preparation, they can scope faster and act with more confidence.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:54.380
Identity often deserves early attention because cloud access is identity-driven.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.360
Compromised users, service accounts, tokens, and access keys can affect many services.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.580
Storage, workloads,

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.600
and network configuration also require careful analysis because containment actions can

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.180
preserve evidence or destroy it depending on how they are performed.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.280
The goal is not only to stop the immediate incident.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.300
The goal is to preserve evidence, understand impact, restore trusted service,

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.720
communicate responsibly, and reduce recurrence.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:28.380
Cloud response must balance urgency with documentation, legal and privacy coordination,

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.720
business continuity, and safe recovery sequencing.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:37.300
The practical takeaway is to make cloud response observable, authorized, and repeatable.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.240
Know your scope, know your logs, know your identities, preserve what matters,

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.260
contain with care, recover from trusted sources,

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:50.680
and convert lessons learned into better architecture, detections, runbooks, and training.
